und truth)\n- **S1 \u2013 single confounder:** U \u2192 X1, U \u2192 X2; no observed edge. True answer: no directed edge; one latent common cause.\n- **S2 \u2013 multiple confounders:** U1 \u2192 {X1, X2}, U2 \u2192 {X3, X4} (observed variables otherwise disconnected).\n- **S3 \u2013 edge + confounder:** U \u2192 X1, U \u2192 X2, AND X1 \u2192 X2. Tests whether the direct edge is oriented while U is still detected.\n- **S4 \u2013 structured:** a chain with shared latents, e.g., X1 \u2192 X2 \u2192 X3 plus U1 \u2192 {X1, X3}, with an additional sibling X4 sharing U1 \u21d2 U1 affects \u2265 3 observed children. Tests partial order amid multiple latent causes.\n\nEach scenario gets sibling-support variants (U affects exactly the tested pair vs. \u22653 observed children) \u2014 this directly probes the S3/S1 separation boundary (Section 8.2).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Parameter grid (planned factorial; all cells defaulted unless a sweep axis is active)\n\n| Axis | Values | Default | Rationale (one line each) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| p (observed vars) | {4, 6, 10} | 4 | consistency/scalability of order recovery and nonparametric regression cost |\n| n (sample size) | {500, 1000, 2000, 5000} | 2000 | finite-sample behavior and n-consistency slope |\n| k (unobserved common causes) | scenario-matched {1, 2, 3} | 1\u20132 | detects whether CAM-UV's latent grouping degrades with the number of confounders |\n| Model family | nonlinear additive (default); Gaussian \u03b5 probe; near-linear non-Gaussian | nonlinear | isolates the identifiability driver (nonlinearity vs. non-Gaussianity) |\n| SNR_conf | {0.2, 1, 5} | 1 | degradation curve vs. confounder strength |\n| Seeds/runs | 50 per cell | 50 | stable means/CIs; fixed seed registry for reproducibility |\n\nExplicit note: the full cross-product is the *planned* grid; the default cell (p=4, n=2000, k=1\u20132, nonlinear+non-Gaussian, SNR_conf=1, 50 seeds) is the design's center point. Sweeps perturb one axis at a time off the default. Feasibility of this grid (runtime) is estimated in Section 14.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Applying CAM-UV (observed variables only)\n\n1. Feed only the n\u00d7p observed data matrix to the CAM-UV procedure.\n2. For each variable pair, the algorithm regresses candidate-effect Y on candidate-cause X (smooth nonparametric regression) and measures residual dependence via nonparametric MI (or HSIC; estimator = working assumption) against a permutation null with a sample-size-aware significance threshold.\n3. Output: estimated causal order over observed variables + detected unobserved common causes, compared to ground truth.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Comparators and controls\n\n- **Standard CAM (no hidden-variable handling) \u2014 core ablation:** the same additive-noise family but assumes no latents; shows the bias CAM-UV removes when confounders exist.\n- **LiNGAM / DirectLiNGAM \u2014 core additive-noise direction control:** shares the residual-independence principle but assumes linear-non-Gaussian; isolates where nonlinearity matters (cross-validates S3 and the near-linear cells) and anchors the non-Gaussianity framing note.\n- **Na\u00efve correlation / minimum-residual-variance baseline \u2014 core sanity baseline:** \"direction\" decided by sign/magnitude of correlation or lower regression residual variance; demonstrates why residual *independence* (not dependence strength or correlation) is the correct signal.\n- **FCI \u2014 optional extension, clearly labeled out-of-scope for the core set:** a constraint-based, latent-variable-aware family for a complementary conditional-independence view; not run in the primary analysis.\n\nWhy each is a meaningful control: CAM (mechanism identical, missing latents), LiNGAM (same principle, wrong functional form), and the correlation baseline (no independence machinery at all) form a monotone series isolating exactly which properties of CAM-UV produce correct discovery under latent confounding.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evaluation metrics and analysis plan\n\n- **Metric A (primary) \u2014 direction-recovery accuracy:** fraction of (i) directed observed edges whose orientation matches ground truth and (ii) confounded pairs correctly labeled \"no edge, latent present\" (S1/S2). Directly answers the research question.\n- **Metric B (primary) \u2014 causal-order accuracy:** fraction of correctly recovered topological order of observed variables (plus Kendall-\u03c4 agreement) on S4.\n- **Secondary \u2014 latent-confounder sensitivity/specificity:** true U's flagged vs. hallucinated ones; **edge precision/recall** on both directed edges and confounding relations.\n- **Analysis structure:** mean \u00b1 95% CI over the 50 seeds per cell; curves over the swept axis vs. n and vs. SNR_conf; paired same-seed comparison of CAM-UV vs. each baseline (simple paired difference, no exotic inference). Primary = Metric A/B in the default cell and the SNR_conf sweep; secondary = precision/recall, sensitivity/specificity, and the Gaussian probe cell.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumed CAM-UV decision rule and the S1-vs-S3 separation analysis\n\nDefine for a pair (X, Y): D(X\u2192Y) = dependence between X and the residual of regressing Y on X (permutation-test p-value / estimated MI); symmetric D(Y\u2192X). **Assumed decision rule** (design assumption, pinned to the reference implementation in Section 14):\n\n- D(X\u2192Y) \u2248 0, D(Y\u2192X) > 0 \u21d2 X \u2192 Y (directed edge, direction X\u2192Y).\n- Both \u2248 0 \u21d2 no edge and no shared latent (independence/screened off).\n- **Both D(X\u2192Y), D(Y\u2192X) > 0 \u21d2 latent common cause flagged; a second, full-set step then decides S1 vs S3.**\n\n**Why pairs alone cannot separate S1 from S3:** In S1 (U\u2192X1, U\u2192X2, no edge) both regressions inherit residual dependence through U \u21d2 both directions fail. In S3 (X\u2192Y plus U\u2192X, U\u2192Y), the structural equation Y = f(X) + g(U) + \u03b5_Y means the residual after regressing Y on X still depends on X through the U-pathway (and the reverse direction fails trivially) \u21d2 **both directions fail again**. The pairwise residual-dependence pattern is therefore identical; pairwise tests are information-theoretically ambiguous here.\n\n**Assumed separation mechanism (full-set step):** CAM-UV must move beyond pairs. Two complementary routes are assumed in this design: (i) *multi-indicator confounder removal* \u2014 if U affects \u22653 observed children, regress candidate effect Y on the sibling indicators and test whether residual-Y-given-siblings remains dependent on X; dependence surviving sibling conditioning indicates a genuine X\u2192Y edge, absence indicates pure confounding; (ii) *order-search/sink logic* \u2014 within the iterative order recovery, a genuine sink X_j is one for which, once latent-mediated dependence is accounted for, no remaining variable's residual depends on X_j. The experiment's S3/S4 scenarios therefore include sibling-support variants (U with exactly 2 vs \u22653 observed children) so this step has leverage.\n\n**Expected failure mode (the boundary the design probes):** when U affects exactly the pair under test and no third indicator exists, the design expects CAM-UV to be unable to separate S3 from S1 for that pair \u2014 both patterns are \"latent flagged\" and edge presence becomes unidentifiable at that support. The design therefore predicts degraded S3 edge detection specifically at minimum (two-child) latent support, and treats that prediction as a testable boundary rather than a bug.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Robustness analyses\n\n- **Noise distribution (secondary identifiability probe):** Gaussian \u03b5 cell \u2014 empirically probes identifiability; expected degradation grows as mechanisms approach linear (framing note, Section 1); non-Gaussian cell remains the default.\n- **Near-linear non-Gaussian cell:** expected agreement with LiNGAM; anchors the nonlinearity/non-Gaussianity framing.\n- **n-scaling:** accuracy vs. n (consistency slope), primary for the finite-sample behavior claim.\n- **SNR_conf sweep:** the main degradation curve.\n- Optional small model-family check: polynomial vs. sigmoid vs. spline f/g \u2014 guards against family-specific artifacts.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Analysis of analysis: controls, sanity, and anti-artifact checks\n\n- **Ground-truth sanity (Section 3):** noise-free (\u03c3\u21920) residuals must show the assumed dependence pattern empirically on the generated data, pre-registered before running discovery \u2014 ensures faithfulness (no accidental cancellation).\n- **Permutation-null significance:** dependence decisions made vs. a permutation null at fixed \u03b1 (e.g., 0.05) with a small Bonferroni/step-down correction across the pair set \u2014 guards against multiple-testing inflation of hallucinated latents.\n- **Same-seed paired comparisons** across methods \u2014 removes seed variance when comparing CAM-UV to controls.\n- **Reproducibility:** fixed seed registry (per scenario \u00d7 cell \u00d7 replication); all hyperparameters logged; reference implementation version pinned.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Consolidated assumptions vs. source-supported facts\n\n- **Source-supported:** observed-only input; output = order + detected unobserved common causes; residual-independence asymmetry as the workhorse signal; additive-noise/nonlinear/non-Gaussian/full-set identifiability framework (at the standard-literature level).\n- **Design assumptions:** DGP families and SNR definition; scenario set and sibling-support variants; grid values and 50-seed replication; estimator choice (MI vs. HSIC), kernels, thresholds; the Section 8 decision rule and full-set separation mechanism; runtime estimates in Section 14.\n- **Paper-dependent items to re-validate before any execution:** exact identifiability theorem and minimal conditions; the precise latent-detection step (S1 vs S3 separation); default hyperparameters.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Uncertainties and limitations\n\n1. CAM-UV paper specifics could not be re-fetched offline (network disabled) \u2014 the exact theorem, estimator, and default parameters are reconstructed from prior knowledge and are flagged for re-validation against the paper/reference implementation.\n2. The S1-vs-S3 separation mechanism (Section 8) is the design's single most assumption-heavy element; the sibling-support boundary design directly tests it.\n3. Choice of nonparametric dependence measure may affect small-n behavior (sensitivity note, not a fixed result).\n4. No experiment was executed; all \"expected\" statements are predictions, and the deliverable remains a design.\n\n---\n\n## 13. References\n- B\u00fchlmann, Peters, Ernst \u2014 *CAM: Causal additive models, high-dimensional order search and penalized regression* (Ann. Statist. 2014).\n- Hoyer, Janzing, Mooij, Peters, Sch\u00f6lkopf \u2014 *Nonlinear causal discovery with additive noise models* (NeurIPS 2008 / 2005 manuscript) \u2014 nonlinearity as identifiability driver.\n- Shimizu, Hoyer, Hyv\u00e4rinen, Kerminen \u2014 *A linear non-Gaussian acyclic model for causal discovery* (LiNGAM; JMLR 2006).\n- CAM-UV paper (reference implementation + description) \u2014 to be pinned by arXiv ID/version at execution time (offline verification not possible here).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Feasibility appendix (estimates only \u2014 nothing was run)\n\n**Requirements if executed:** Python with numpy, scipy, scikit-learn (nonparametric regression for the residual step) plus the **authors' CAM-UV reference implementation** (pinned version). System Python is 3.14 with no preinstalled packages and no network, so execution would require an environment provisioning step first.\n\n**Runtime model (per dataset / per replication):** cost \u2248 O(pair_count \u00d7 (regression + MI estimation)) + the full-set latent search. For the reference implementation in the default cell (p=4, n=2000, 1\u20132 latents), expect roughly **0.5\u20133 minutes** per dataset; p=10 / n=5000 / k=3 cells plausibly reach **several minutes to ~15 min** per dataset (order-of-magnitude engineering estimates, to be replaced by a 5-cell pilot timing if ever executed).\n\n**Per-cell and total wall-clock estimate:** 50 seeds / cell \u21d2 default cells \u2248 0.5\u20132.5 h; heavy cells (p=10, k=3, n=5000) \u2248 up to ~12 h per cell. The full planned factorial (\u22484 scenarios \u00d7 sibling variants \u00d7 4 n \u00d7 3 SNR \u00d7 small family check \u2248 2.5\u20133\u00d710^3 dataset-replications) plausibly totals **on the order of 2\u20138 core-weeks** on a single core \u2014 or ~1\u20132 days on ~100 cores (the target environment advertises 224 CPUs). These are defensibility estimates to justify the grid; they are not measured results.\n\n**If total budget were an active constraint:** the grid contracts by running the n and SNR_conf sweeps only on the default cell (p=4; k=1\u20132) and restricting p=10/k=3 to the default n/SNR, roughly halving total cost while preserving every metric and every robustness axis.\n", "evidence": "Verified /workspace contains only task.json (no track.md on disk, no datasets, no code, no packages; pip unavailable, bare system Python 3.14, network disabled), confirming the task is a fully self-contained written-design deliverable. Source-supported CAM-UV facts and all design assumptions are separated in Section 1 and consolidated in Section 11; the no-execution status is stated at the top, in Section 12, and in Section 14. Reviewer refinements folded in: FCI demoted to clearly-labeled optional (Section 6); full planned factorial with a default cell and one-line rationale per axis (Section 4); Gaussian cell reframed as a secondary identifiability probe with the Hoyer 2005 / LiNGAM 2006 nonlinearity-vs-non-Gaussianity framing note (Section 1, 9, 13); dedicated S1-vs-S3 separation analysis with an assumed decision rule and expected failure mode (Section 8); feasibility as an appendix with runtime/total-cost estimates (Section 14).", "assumptions": ["CAM-UV takes observed variables only and returns causal order plus detected unobserved common causes; the residual-independence asymmetry (cause independent of effect's residual under additive noise) is its workhorse signal.", "Exact algorithm internals (dependence estimator MI vs HSIC, kernels, thresholds, latent-grouping search) are working assumptions pinned to the authors' reference implementation.", "DGP families (polynomial/sigmoid/spline), SNR_conf definition, scenario set S1-S4 plus sibling-support variants, grid values, and 50-seed replication are design choices.", "The S1-vs-S3 separation mechanism (multi-indicator conditioning and order-search/sink logic) is the design's most assumption-heavy element and is testable via sibling-support variants.", "Identifiability conditions (additive noise, nonlinear mechanisms, faithfulness, non-Gaussianity primarily in the near-linear regime) are conditions the design satisfies, with the Gaussian cell as an empirical probe.", "Runtime and wall-clock figures in the feasibility appendix are order-of-magnitude engineering estimates for defensibility, not measured results."], "uncertainties": ["CAM-UV paper specifics (exact identifiability theorem, latent-detection step, default hyperparameters) could not be re-fetched offline; flagged for re-validation against the paper/reference implementation before any execution.", "The pairwise test is information-theoretically ambiguous for S1 vs S3 (identical dual-dependence pattern); separation depends on the full-set step and the sibling-support boundary design.", "Choice of nonparametric dependence measure may affect small-n behavior; noted as a sensitivity item.", "No experiment was executed; all expected behaviors are predictions, and the deliverable remains a design with no claimed outcomes."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-9adf2c5bbd45469f8e3a43e0ebd53df2", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design \u2014 Causal Discovery with Unobserved Common Causes Using CAM-UV\n\n**Question addressed:** How can you discover causal relationships in data with unobserved common causes using the CAM-UV algorithm?\n\n**Status (explicit):** This is a **design-only deliverable**. No experiment was run; nothing was executed; no outcome is claimed. All expected behaviors below are predictions to be tested by the design. A feasibility appendix (Section 14) estimates what an execution would require (estimates only).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Terminology and source-supported basis (separated from working assumptions)\n\n| Claim | Status |\n|---|---|\n| CAM-UV extends the Causal Additive Model (B\u00fchlmann et al. 2014) to settings with unobserved common causes (latent variables that directly cause \u22652 observed variables). | Source-supported (from my prior knowledge of the published CAM-UV work; re-validate against the paper before execution). |\n| Input is observed variables only; output is an estimated causal order/direction among observed variables plus detected unobserved common causes. | Source-supported. |\n| Working signal: under additive noise, if X \u2192 Y then the residual of Y's regression on X is independent of X; the reverse regression and true common-cause confounding produce dependence (the residual-independence asymmetry). | Source-supported. |\n| Identifiability rests on additive noise, smooth (nonlinear) mechanisms, a faithfulness-type no-cancellation assumption, and (in the near-linear regime) non-Gaussianity of disturbances. | Source-supported at the level of the standard additive-noise literature; exact minimal conditions treated as assumptions (see Section 12) pending paper access. |\n| Exact search procedure, nonparametric dependence estimator (MI vs. HSIC; kernel/binning), hyperparameters, and the latent-grouping heuristic. | **Working assumptions** (pinned to the authors' reference implementation in Section 14). |\n| All scenarios, DGP families, parameter grid, SNR definition, and replication counts. | **Design assumptions/choices** (this document). |\n\n**Framing note on identifiability (per review):** In additive-noise models, the driver of direction identifiability under the cause\u2013residual-independence criterion is *nonlinearity* of the mechanism (Hoyer et al. 2005; a nonlinear map X\u2192Y permits X \u22a5 \u03b5 even when \u03b5 is Gaussian). Non-Gaussianity becomes the decisive workhorse specifically when mechanisms approach *linear* (LiNGAM; Shimizu et al. 2006). This design therefore treats **non-Gaussianity as required primarily when mechanisms approach linear**, and presents the Gaussian-disturbance cell as an *empirical probe* of CAM-UV's identifiability \u2014 not a presumed failure. This is a framing note, not a fixed requirement.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Objectives and hypotheses\n\n- **O1 (primary):** Show that with observed variables only, CAM-UV recovers the correct causal order/orientation among observed variables even when unobserved common causes are present.\n- **O2 (primary):** Show that CAM-UV detects true unobserved common causes without systematically hallucinating non-existent ones.\n- **O3 (secondary):** Characterize how performance degrades as confounder strength increases and with disturbance Gaussianity; establish the n-consistency slope.\n\nTestable predictions (H1\u2013H4): H1 \u2014 CAM-UV direction accuracy on confounded observed structures (S3/S4) exceeds the naive correlation baseline and standard CAM in the presence of latents. H2 \u2014 In pure-confounding pairs (S1), CAM-UV flags \"latent present, no edge\" rather than fabricating a direction. H3 \u2014 Performance degrades gracefully with SNR_conf (not cliff-like). H4 \u2014 The Gaussian-disturbance probe shows degradation that grows as mechanisms approach linear.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data-generating process (known ground truth)\n\nDraw a DAG over observed X_1..X_p and unobserved U_1..U_k with a fixed topological order, then generate independently per replication:\n\n> X_j = \u03a3_{i\u2208Pa_j} f_ij(X_i) + \u03a3_{u\u2208Conf_j} g_uj(U_u) + \u03b5_j\n\n- f, g: smooth nonlinear \u2014 default polynomial deg 2\u20133 (plus sigmoid and spline variants in a small model-family check); coefficients drawn from fixed random registry.\n- \u03b5_j ~ Laplace(0, \u03c3_j^2) by default (non-Gaussian); \u03c3_j set by SNR (below).\n- U_u drawn iid from a fixed distribution (e.g., Laplace), scaled to target confounder strength.\n- Faithfulness: f/g coefficients drawn generically; a noise-free ground-truth sanity check (Section 10.4) verifies residual non-independence empirically so no accidental cancellation is in force.\n- Confounder strength: SNR_conf = Var(confounder contribution to X_j)/Var(noise \u03b5_j). Default 1 (\"medium\"); swept {0.2, 1, 5}.\n\n### 3.1 Ground-truth scenarios (all have known, verifiable ground truth)\n- **S1 \u2013 single confounder:** U \u2192 X1, U \u2192 X2; no observed edge. True answer: no directed edge; one latent common cause.\n- **S2 \u2013 multiple confounders:** U1 \u2192 {X1, X2}, U2 \u2192 {X3, X4} (observed variables otherwise disconnected).\n- **S3 \u2013 edge + confounder:** U \u2192 X1, U \u2192 X2, AND X1 \u2192 X2. Tests whether the direct edge is oriented while U is still detected.\n- **S4 \u2013 structured:** a chain with shared latents, e.g., X1 \u2192 X2 \u2192 X3 plus U1 \u2192 {X1, X3}, with an additional sibling X4 sharing U1 \u21d2 U1 affects \u2265 3 observed children. Tests partial order amid multiple latent causes.\n\nEach scenario gets sibling-support variants (U affects exactly the tested pair vs. \u22653 observed children) \u2014 this directly probes the S3/S1 separation boundary (Section 8.2).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Parameter grid (planned factorial; all cells defaulted unless a sweep axis is active)\n\n| Axis | Values | Default | Rationale (one line each) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| p (observed vars) | {4, 6, 10} | 4 | consistency/scalability of order recovery and nonparametric regression cost |\n| n (sample size) | {500, 1000, 2000, 5000} | 2000 | finite-sample behavior and n-consistency slope |\n| k (unobserved common causes) | scenario-matched {1, 2, 3} | 1\u20132 | detects whether CAM-UV's latent grouping degrades with the number of confounders |\n| Model family | nonlinear additive (default); Gaussian \u03b5 probe; near-linear non-Gaussian | nonlinear | isolates the identifiability driver (nonlinearity vs. non-Gaussianity) |\n| SNR_conf | {0.2, 1, 5} | 1 | degradation curve vs. confounder strength |\n| Seeds/runs | 50 per cell | 50 | stable means/CIs; fixed seed registry for reproducibility |\n\nExplicit note: the full cross-product is the *planned* grid; the default cell (p=4, n=2000, k=1\u20132, nonlinear+non-Gaussian, SNR_conf=1, 50 seeds) is the design's center point. Sweeps perturb one axis at a time off the default. Feasibility of this grid (runtime) is estimated in Section 14.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Applying CAM-UV (observed variables only)\n\n1. Feed only the n\u00d7p observed data matrix to the CAM-UV procedure.\n2. For each variable pair, the algorithm regresses candidate-effect Y on candidate-cause X (smooth nonparametric regression) and measures residual dependence via nonparametric MI (or HSIC; estimator = working assumption) against a permutation null with a sample-size-aware significance threshold.\n3. Output: estimated causal order over observed variables + detected unobserved common causes, compared to ground truth.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Comparators and controls\n\n- **Standard CAM (no hidden-variable handling) \u2014 core ablation:** the same additive-noise family but assumes no latents; shows the bias CAM-UV removes when confounders exist.\n- **LiNGAM / DirectLiNGAM \u2014 core additive-noise direction control:** shares the residual-independence principle but assumes linear-non-Gaussian; isolates where nonlinearity matters (cross-validates S3 and the near-linear cells) and anchors the non-Gaussianity framing note.\n- **Na\u00efve correlation / minimum-residual-variance baseline \u2014 core sanity baseline:** \"direction\" decided by sign/magnitude of correlation or lower regression residual variance; demonstrates why residual *independence* (not dependence strength or correlation) is the correct signal.\n- **FCI \u2014 optional extension, clearly labeled out-of-scope for the core set:** a constraint-based, latent-variable-aware family for a complementary conditional-independence view; not run in the primary analysis.\n\nWhy each is a meaningful control: CAM (mechanism identical, missing latents), LiNGAM (same principle, wrong functional form), and the correlation baseline (no independence machinery at all) form a monotone series isolating exactly which properties of CAM-UV produce correct discovery under latent confounding.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evaluation metrics and analysis plan\n\n- **Metric A (primary) \u2014 direction-recovery accuracy:** fraction of (i) directed observed edges whose orientation matches ground truth and (ii) confounded pairs correctly labeled \"no edge, latent present\" (S1/S2). Directly answers the research question.\n- **Metric B (primary) \u2014 causal-order accuracy:** fraction of correctly recovered topological order of observed variables (plus Kendall-\u03c4 agreement) on S4.\n- **Secondary \u2014 latent-confounder sensitivity/specificity:** true U's flagged vs. hallucinated ones; **edge precision/recall** on both directed edges and confounding relations.\n- **Analysis structure:** mean \u00b1 95% CI over the 50 seeds per cell; curves over the swept axis vs. n and vs. SNR_conf; paired same-seed comparison of CAM-UV vs. each baseline (simple paired difference, no exotic inference). Primary = Metric A/B in the default cell and the SNR_conf sweep; secondary = precision/recall, sensitivity/specificity, and the Gaussian probe cell.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumed CAM-UV decision rule and the S1-vs-S3 separation analysis\n\nDefine for a pair (X, Y): D(X\u2192Y) = dependence between X and the residual of regressing Y on X (permutation-test p-value / estimated MI); symmetric D(Y\u2192X). **Assumed decision rule** (design assumption, pinned to the reference implementation in Section 14):\n\n- D(X\u2192Y) \u2248 0, D(Y\u2192X) > 0 \u21d2 X \u2192 Y (directed edge, direction X\u2192Y).\n- Both \u2248 0 \u21d2 no edge and no shared latent (independence/screened off).\n- **Both D(X\u2192Y), D(Y\u2192X) > 0 \u21d2 latent common cause flagged; a second, full-set step then decides S1 vs S3.**\n\n**Why pairs alone cannot separate S1 from S3:** In S1 (U\u2192X1, U\u2192X2, no edge) both regressions inherit residual dependence through U \u21d2 both directions fail. In S3 (X\u2192Y plus U\u2192X, U\u2192Y), the structural equation Y = f(X) + g(U) + \u03b5_Y means the residual after regressing Y on X still depends on X through the U-pathway (and the reverse direction fails trivially) \u21d2 **both directions fail again**. The pairwise residual-dependence pattern is therefore identical; pairwise tests are information-theoretically ambiguous here.\n\n**Assumed separation mechanism (full-set step):** CAM-UV must move beyond pairs. Two complementary routes are assumed in this design: (i) *multi-indicator confounder removal* \u2014 if U affects \u22653 observed children, regress candidate effect Y on the sibling indicators and test whether residual-Y-given-siblings remains dependent on X; dependence surviving sibling conditioning indicates a genuine X\u2192Y edge, absence indicates pure confounding; (ii) *order-search/sink logic* \u2014 within the iterative order recovery, a genuine sink X_j is one for which, once latent-mediated dependence is accounted for, no remaining variable's residual depends on X_j. The experiment's S3/S4 scenarios therefore include sibling-support variants (U with exactly 2 vs \u22653 observed children) so this step has leverage.\n\n**Expected failure mode (the boundary the design probes):** when U affects exactly the pair under test and no third indicator exists, the design expects CAM-UV to be unable to separate S3 from S1 for that pair \u2014 both patterns are \"latent flagged\" and edge presence becomes unidentifiable at that support. The design therefore predicts degraded S3 edge detection specifically at minimum (two-child) latent support, and treats that prediction as a testable boundary rather than a bug.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Robustness analyses\n\n- **Noise distribution (secondary identifiability probe):** Gaussian \u03b5 cell \u2014 empirically probes identifiability; expected degradation grows as mechanisms approach linear (framing note, Section 1); non-Gaussian cell remains the default.\n- **Near-linear non-Gaussian cell:** expected agreement with LiNGAM; anchors the nonlinearity/non-Gaussianity framing.\n- **n-scaling:** accuracy vs. n (consistency slope), primary for the finite-sample behavior claim.\n- **SNR_conf sweep:** the main degradation curve.\n- Optional small model-family check: polynomial vs. sigmoid vs. spline f/g \u2014 guards against family-specific artifacts.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Analysis of analysis: controls, sanity, and anti-artifact checks\n\n- **Ground-truth sanity (Section 3):** noise-free (\u03c3\u21920) residuals must show the assumed dependence pattern empirically on the generated data, pre-registered before running discovery \u2014 ensures faithfulness (no accidental cancellation).\n- **Permutation-null significance:** dependence decisions made vs. a permutation null at fixed \u03b1 (e.g., 0.05) with a small Bonferroni/step-down correction across the pair set \u2014 guards against multiple-testing inflation of hallucinated latents.\n- **Same-seed paired comparisons** across methods \u2014 removes seed variance when comparing CAM-UV to controls.\n- **Reproducibility:** fixed seed registry (per scenario \u00d7 cell \u00d7 replication); all hyperparameters logged; reference implementation version pinned.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Consolidated assumptions vs. source-supported facts\n\n- **Source-supported:** observed-only input; output = order + detected unobserved common causes; residual-independence asymmetry as the workhorse signal; additive-noise/nonlinear/non-Gaussian/full-set identifiability framework (at the standard-literature level).\n- **Design assumptions:** DGP families and SNR definition; scenario set and sibling-support variants; grid values and 50-seed replication; estimator choice (MI vs. HSIC), kernels, thresholds; the Section 8 decision rule and full-set separation mechanism; runtime estimates in Section 14.\n- **Paper-dependent items to re-validate before any execution:** exact identifiability theorem and minimal conditions; the precise latent-detection step (S1 vs S3 separation); default hyperparameters.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Uncertainties and limitations\n\n1. CAM-UV paper specifics could not be re-fetched offline (network disabled) \u2014 the exact theorem, estimator, and default parameters are reconstructed from prior knowledge and are flagged for re-validation against the paper/reference implementation.\n2. The S1-vs-S3 separation mechanism (Section 8) is the design's single most assumption-heavy element; the sibling-support boundary design directly tests it.\n3. Choice of nonparametric dependence measure may affect small-n behavior (sensitivity note, not a fixed result).\n4. No experiment was executed; all \"expected\" statements are predictions, and the deliverable remains a design.\n\n---\n\n## 13. References\n- B\u00fchlmann, Peters, Ernst \u2014 *CAM: Causal additive models, high-dimensional order search and penalized regression* (Ann. Statist. 2014).\n- Hoyer, Janzing, Mooij, Peters, Sch\u00f6lkopf \u2014 *Nonlinear causal discovery with additive noise models* (NeurIPS 2008 / 2005 manuscript) \u2014 nonlinearity as identifiability driver.\n- Shimizu, Hoyer, Hyv\u00e4rinen, Kerminen \u2014 *A linear non-Gaussian acyclic model for causal discovery* (LiNGAM; JMLR 2006).\n- CAM-UV paper (reference implementation + description) \u2014 to be pinned by arXiv ID/version at execution time (offline verification not possible here).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Feasibility appendix (estimates only \u2014 nothing was run)\n\n**Requirements if executed:** Python with numpy, scipy, scikit-learn (nonparametric regression for the residual step) plus the **authors' CAM-UV reference implementation** (pinned version). System Python is 3.14 with no preinstalled packages and no network, so execution would require an environment provisioning step first.\n\n**Runtime model (per dataset / per replication):** cost \u2248 O(pair_count \u00d7 (regression + MI estimation)) + the full-set latent search. For the reference implementation in the default cell (p=4, n=2000, 1\u20132 latents), expect roughly **0.5\u20133 minutes** per dataset; p=10 / n=5000 / k=3 cells plausibly reach **several minutes to ~15 min** per dataset (order-of-magnitude engineering estimates, to be replaced by a 5-cell pilot timing if ever executed).\n\n**Per-cell and total wall-clock estimate:** 50 seeds / cell \u21d2 default cells \u2248 0.5\u20132.5 h; heavy cells (p=10, k=3, n=5000) \u2248 up to ~12 h per cell. The full planned factorial (\u22484 scenarios \u00d7 sibling variants \u00d7 4 n \u00d7 3 SNR \u00d7 small family check \u2248 2.5\u20133\u00d710^3 dataset-replications) plausibly totals **on the order of 2\u20138 core-weeks** on a single core \u2014 or ~1\u20132 days on ~100 cores (the target environment advertises 224 CPUs). These are defensibility estimates to justify the grid; they are not measured results.\n\n**If total budget were an active constraint:** the grid contracts by running the n and SNR_conf sweeps only on the default cell (p=4; k=1\u20132) and restricting p=10/k=3 to the default n/SNR, roughly halving total cost while preserving every metric and every robustness axis.\n", "evidence": "Verified /workspace contains only task.json (no track.md on disk, no datasets, no code, no packages; pip unavailable, bare system Python 3.14, network disabled), confirming the task is a fully self-contained written-design deliverable. Source-supported CAM-UV facts and all design assumptions are separated in Section 1 and consolidated in Section 11; the no-execution status is stated at the top, in Section 12, and in Section 14. Reviewer refinements folded in: FCI demoted to clearly-labeled optional (Section 6); full planned factorial with a default cell and one-line rationale per axis (Section 4); Gaussian cell reframed as a secondary identifiability probe with the Hoyer 2005 / LiNGAM 2006 nonlinearity-vs-non-Gaussianity framing note (Section 1, 9, 13); dedicated S1-vs-S3 separation analysis with an assumed decision rule and expected failure mode (Section 8); feasibility as an appendix with runtime/total-cost estimates (Section 14).", "assumptions": ["CAM-UV takes observed variables only and returns causal order plus detected unobserved common causes; the residual-independence asymmetry (cause independent of effect's residual under additive noise) is its workhorse signal.", "Exact algorithm internals (dependence estimator MI vs HSIC, kernels, thresholds, latent-grouping search) are working assumptions pinned to the authors' reference implementation.", "DGP families (polynomial/sigmoid/spline), SNR_conf definition, scenario set S1-S4 plus sibling-support variants, grid values, and 50-seed replication are design choices.", "The S1-vs-S3 separation mechanism (multi-indicator conditioning and order-search/sink logic) is the design's most assumption-heavy element and is testable via sibling-support variants.", "Identifiability conditions (additive noise, nonlinear mechanisms, faithfulness, non-Gaussianity primarily in the near-linear regime) are conditions the design satisfies, with the Gaussian cell as an empirical probe.", "Runtime and wall-clock figures in the feasibility appendix are order-of-magnitude engineering estimates for defensibility, not measured results."], "uncertainties": ["CAM-UV paper specifics (exact identifiability theorem, latent-detection step, default hyperparameters) could not be re-fetched offline; flagged for re-validation against the paper/reference implementation before any execution.", "The pairwise test is information-theoretically ambiguous for S1 vs S3 (identical dual-dependence pattern); separation depends on the full-set step and the sibling-support boundary design.", "Choice of nonparametric dependence measure may affect small-n behavior; noted as a sensitivity item.", "No experiment was executed; all expected behaviors are predictions, and the deliverable remains a design with no claimed outcomes."]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
